WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.424
An active browser can open a WebSocket and exchange
messages in both directions while the connection

00:00:06.424 --> 00:00:07.323
remains alive.

00:00:07.323 --> 00:00:13.489
Long polling instead leaves an HTTP request pending
until data or a timeout arrives, then starts

00:00:13.489 --> 00:00:14.517
another request.

00:00:14.517 --> 00:00:18.371
Both permit an update to arrive without a fresh user
action.

00:00:18.371 --> 00:00:24.217
Their latency and operational behavior differ from
repeatedly making short periodic polling

00:00:24.217 --> 00:00:24.795
requests.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:30.653
A distributed notification service must find the
worker holding each live client connection.

00:00:30.653 --> 00:00:37.016
Other backend services should send a logical user
notification rather than guess the physical socket

00:00:37.016 --> 00:00:37.397
owner.

00:00:37.397 --> 00:00:43.505
A registry connects user and installation identities
to provider destinations or live connection

00:00:43.505 --> 00:00:44.269
identifiers.

00:00:44.269 --> 00:00:50.567
This allows the notification service to serve
multiple devices and browser tabs without
duplicating

00:00:50.567 --> 00:00:52.921
routing logic across domain services.
